            <ArticleTitle>[Results after laser epithelial keratectomileusis for myopia].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>65-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study the results of laser epithelial keratectomileusis (LASEK) for myopia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">LASEK was performed with the Nidek EC-5000 excimer laser. 39 patients (78 eyes) with a refraction of -1.50 to -12.00 diopters were treated and followed for 3-12 months. Postoperrative pain, uncorrected visual acuity (UCVA), refraction, corneal haze, and surgical preference were examined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">During 3-12 months follow-up, 96.2% UCVA were above 0.5, 92.3% eyes were at least 1.0 in UCVA. The last refraction were whthin +1.00 D and -1.00 D in 18 eyes except 4 eyes. The II grade haze was found in four eye.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">LASEK was one of methods to treat eyes with myopia safely and effectively, but also studied in operation, experiment, and further effect.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
